WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.549
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:15.751
Unit six: Supply Chain Theory. Today you will evaluate efficiency versus resilience with trade-off contrast.

4
00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:18.668
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.959
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:25.073
Supply chain.

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:30.475
Supply chain means the network that moves a product from raw materials to customers.

8
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:34.511
A single factory fire disrupted the entire supply chain.

9
00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:37.781
Your turn. Say: Supply chain.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:41.867
Supply chain.

11
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:43.734
Logistics.

12
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.781
Logistics means the organisation of transport and storage of goods.

13
00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:52.846
Better logistics cut delivery time to Siem Reap by a day.

14
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.439
Your turn. Say: Logistics.

15
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.510
Logistics.

16
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.750
Inventory management.

17
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.166
Inventory management means controlling the amount of stock held.

18
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.549
Poor inventory management left shelves empty before Pchum Ben.

19
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.162
Your turn. Say: Inventory management.

20
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.791
Inventory management.

21
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.259
Supplier relationship.

22
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:28.698
Supplier relationship means the long-term connection between a buyer and its suppliers.

23
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:33.338
Strong supplier relationships helped them secure fabric during shortages.

24
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.241
Your turn. Say: Supplier relationship.

25
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.798
Supplier relationship.

26
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:43.621
Lead time.

27
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:48.102
Lead time means the time between placing an order and receiving it.

28
00:01:48.717 --> 00:01:51.599
The lead time for imported zips is six weeks.

29
00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:54.870
Your turn. Say: Lead time.

30
00:01:57.733 --> 00:01:58.781
Lead time.

31
00:01:59.517 --> 00:02:00.564
Distribution.

32
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:05.488
Distribution means the process of getting products to sellers or customers.

33
00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:09.128
Distribution through village shops reached rural buyers.

34
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.706
Your turn. Say: Distribution.

35
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:16.672
Distribution.

36
00:02:17.417 --> 00:02:18.511
Just-in-time.

37
00:02:19.517 --> 00:02:23.234
Just-in-time means producing or delivering goods only when they are needed.

38
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.022
Just-in-time production keeps warehouse costs low.

39
00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:30.478
Your turn. Say: Just-in-time.

40
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:34.439
Just-in-time.

41
00:02:35.183 --> 00:02:36.335
Resilience.

42
00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.056
Resilience means the ability to recover quickly from disruption.

43
00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.613
Holding extra stock improved the chain's resilience.

44
00:02:45.217 --> 00:02:47.799
Your turn. Say: Resilience.

45
00:02:50.633 --> 00:02:51.901
Resilience.

46
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.374
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.217 --> 00:03:04.722
Visal, during the pandemic many Cambodian garment factories stopped because fabric from abroad didn't arrive. Why were they so exposed?

48
00:03:05.817 --> 00:03:16.399
They relied on just-in-time delivery from a single region. Efficiency and resilience are often a difficult trade-off, and they had chosen pure efficiency.

49
00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:19.750
But holding extra stock costs money.

50
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:30.039
True. Whereas lean inventory saves cash in normal times, it leaves no buffer when shipping is delayed for months.

51
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.138
So what's the smarter approach?

52
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:43.636
Diversify suppliers and keep safety stock of critical materials. It's less efficient on paper, but far more resilient in a crisis.

53
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.451
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.317 --> 00:03:51.859
Grammar focus: Trade-off contrast (efficiency vs resilience).

55
00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:53.415
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:54.117 --> 00:03:57.649
Efficiency and resilience are often a difficult trade-off.

57
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:03.278
Lean inventory saves cash whereas safety stock protects against delays.

58
00:04:04.183 --> 00:04:07.216
The firm cut costs at the expense of flexibility.

59
00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:16.032
Use 'whereas' to contrast two facts in one sentence (comma before 'whereas' is common). 'At the expense of' means a gain came with a loss.

60
00:04:16.783 --> 00:04:20.817
Be careful! Don't say: They grew at the expense from quality.

61
00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:25.916
Say: They grew at the expense of quality.

62
00:04:26.216 --> 00:04:27.449
At the expense of.

63
00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:30.979
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:33.279
Lean or Robust?.

65
00:04:33.579 --> 00:04:41.093
For decades, supply-chain theory celebrated efficiency, encouraging firms to minimise inventory and source from the cheapest supplier.

66
00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:49.359
Just-in-time systems, pioneered by Toyota, reduced waste dramatically, whereas they also removed the buffers that absorb shocks.

67
00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:55.721
The pandemic exposed this weakness when factories worldwide halted because a single component was missing.

68
00:04:56.733 --> 00:05:03.539
Many companies now diversify suppliers and hold strategic stock, accepting higher costs in exchange for resilience.

69
00:05:04.533 --> 00:05:10.980
The modern challenge is therefore not choosing efficiency or resilience, but deciding how much of each a business can afford.

70
00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:15.906
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.526
Lean stock saves money, blank extra stock reduces risk.

72
00:05:20.826 --> 00:05:25.269
A, whereas. B, despite. C, because of.

73
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:31.053
The answer is A: whereas.

74
00:05:32.217 --> 00:05:35.191
The time between ordering and receiving goods is the blank.

75
00:05:35.491 --> 00:05:39.725
A, distribution. B, lead time. C, logistics.

76
00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:45.417
The answer is B: lead time.

77
00:05:46.583 --> 00:05:49.233
Delivering goods only when they are needed is blank.

78
00:05:49.533 --> 00:05:54.115
A, resilience. B, inventory. C, just-in-time.

79
00:05:57.667 --> 00:05:59.852
The answer is C: just-in-time.

80
00:06:01.017 --> 00:06:02.807
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:03.107 --> 00:06:11.277
A, They grew fast at the expense of quality.. B, They grew fast at the expense for quality.. C, They grew fast in the expense of quality..

82
00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:18.516
The answer is A: They grew fast at the expense of quality..

83
00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:23.020
According to the reading, the pandemic exposed the weakness of…

84
00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:28.947
A, holding too much stock. B, systems with no buffers. C, local suppliers.

85
00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.359
The answer is B: systems with no buffers.

86
00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:39.707
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:44.953
Why might a company choose resilience over pure efficiency in its supply chain?

88
00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.668
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:03.968 --> 00:07:42.945
A company might choose resilience because the cost of a complete shutdown is usually far greater than the cost of holding extra stock. Pure efficiency works well when everything runs smoothly, whereas a single delay can halt production for weeks. Cambodian garment factories learned this during the pandemic, when imported fabric stopped arriving. By diversifying suppliers, a firm sacrifices a little profit but protects its customers and workers. I believe that trade-off is wise in an unpredictable world.

90
00:07:43.245 --> 00:07:44.919
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:45.817 --> 00:07:51.882
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and trade-off contrast (efficiency vs resilience).

92
00:07:52.182 --> 00:07:56.774
Today's value is resilience. Prepare for setbacks, and recover from them with strength.

93
00:07:57.074 --> 00:07:59.436
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:59.736 --> 00:08:02.179
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

